<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lydia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lydian kingdom</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lydia was a kingdom in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, famous for its wealth and for early coinage. Its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> around 547 BCE. The conquest brought Lydia and the Greek cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lydia.jpg" alt="Lydia" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ennomus · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Lydia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Notable campaigns included Cyrus's conquest of <b>Lydia</b>, where camels were reportedly deployed to unsettle the Lydian cavalry, the conquest of Egypt (525 BCE), Darius's Scythian campaign (c. 513 BCE), and Xerxes's massive invasion of Greece (480 BCE), which brought victory at Thermopylae and the sack of Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Cyrus the Great conquered <b>Lydia</b> in 546 BCE, Chios, according to Herodotus, submitted to the Achaemenid Empire of its own accord, although the Persians as yet had no fleet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ephesus lived from its position at the western end of routes into the Anatolian interior, above all the road to Sardis, capital of the Kingdom of <b>Lydia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest culture that is identifiably Etruscan dates from about 900 BCE, the Iron Age Villanovan phase, and the consensus of modern scholars is that the Etruscans were an indigenous people, not migrants from <b>Lydia</b> or the Aegean as some Greek writers held.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The war between <b>Lydia</b> and Media reportedly ended when a solar eclipse darkened a battle in 585 BCE, which Greek tradition said Thales of Miletus had predicted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gordium site shows a considerable building programme in the 6th century BCE under Lydian kings including the proverbially rich Croesus, and in Seleucid times Antiochus III settled 2,000 Jewish families from Mesopotamia and Babylon in <b>Lydia</b> and Phrygia with land, tax exemptions and vineyards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
